{"parentUuid":"24b9b4ef-29cf-4f3d-8d4e-874a4d85e04a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch1oS5ahFU24tzwe6vvax","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The keeper's name was Tomas Reyne, and he had tended the lighthouse at Gull Point for thirty-one years. Every evening at dusk he climbed the 144 iron steps, wound the clockwork that turned the great lens, and lit the lamp. Every morning he climbed them again to put it out. The sea never gave him a day off, and neither did the light.\n\nThe station sat alone on a spit of black rock, two miles from the nearest village. Supply boats came once a month. The rest of the time, Tomas had the gulls, the wind, and the long grey hush of the water.\n\nHis wife, Elin, had lived there for the first decade. Then the winters grew too hard for her lungs, and she moved inland to her sister's house in Harrow Bay. She wrote every week. Her letters arrived with the supply boat, folded small and smelling of lavender, and he kept them in a biscuit tin beside his bunk. He wrote back about the weather, the shearwaters, and how the lens had cracked a pane in March. He never wrote that he missed her, because he did not know how to say it on paper, and because she already knew.\n\nOne night in late October, a fog rolled in so thick that Tomas could not see the railing from the gallery. He lit the lamp as always, but the beam only dissolved into the white wall around him. Below, he heard the low moan of a ship's horn. Then another. Somewhere out there, a vessel was feeling its way toward rocks it could not see.\n\nTomas did what he had always done. He kept the light"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"input_transformations":[],"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"type":"assistant","uuid":"9856abac-5ec6-4888-9590-f0873427c512","timestamp":"2026-11-13T23:33:18.508Z","isAbortedMidStream":true,"effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","cwd":"/tmp/c4/w4","sessionId":"c